WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:12.740
In the last few decades, Chile has been the
Cristiano Ronaldo of Latin America: it was

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:17.130
the country with the highest growth, had the
most success, and, sticking with our metaphor

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.240
here, “scored the most goals.”

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.520
Also for many classical liberals, Chile has
been the benchmark for years.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.010
We could say they considered the Chilean model
some sort of dream in a world full of dark

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:29.250
nightmares.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:36.870
And it was true, there were good reasons for
celebration:

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.000
&nbsp;
Chile, which once was the poorest Spanish

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.850
colony in Latin America became the richest
in the region.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:45.680
It became the country with the highest life
expectancy and the country with the best social

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.739
services in the entire region.

00:00:47.739 --> 00:00:49.940
&nbsp;
It is true that inequality is very high in

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:54.350
Chile, but the entire population of Chile
has benefited from the prosperity the country

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.370
has achieved over the past few decades.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:02.020
For the past 25 years, the income of the poorest
people in the country has increased far more

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:06.860
than the income of the wealthiest people - and
their purchasing power has grown six times

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:07.860
larger.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:12.090
All of this compares very favourable to other
Latin-American nations…

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:17.270
As an example: in 1950, the average Chilean
income was around a third of a Venezuelan

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:18.270
income.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.689
In a manner of speaking, Venezuelans were
three times richer.

00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:24.050
&nbsp;
A lot has changed since then and, today, Chileans

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.190
are much richer than Venezuelans.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.289
&nbsp;
They also have a longer life expectancy.

00:01:28.289 --> 00:01:34.810
A Chilean lives an average of eight years
more than a person born in Venezuela.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:41.670
Now, comparing Chile to Venezuela, or to Argentina,
sure makes Chile look good.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:46.050
But when the comparison is made with other,
more distant, countries, like those in Asia

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:50.300
for example, the figures aren't nearly so
nice and, further, it seems as if the country

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.290
has stopped growing, which is worse...

00:01:54.290 --> 00:02:00.989
(THE CHILEAN SLOWDOWN)

00:02:00.989 --> 00:02:04.820
In the last few years, the optimism and success
has come to an end.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.940
&nbsp;
In Santiago, the capital of the country, the

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:11.780
displays of wealth and prosperity have left,
making room instead for demonstrations and

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:12.840
protests.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.670
&nbsp;
Anger and despair have taken over, and it's

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:19.170
only logical...

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:24.120
Economic growth has fallen for the last few
years, and in 2016, unemployment increased,

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.540
while salaries remain frozen.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.900
Further the youth can't find jobs and both
investment and exports have fallen.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:33.530
And the thing is that things aren’t looking
like they are going to get any better any

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:34.800
time soon.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.700
(“In 2016, the Chilean economy grew by 1.5%.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:46.330
The Central Bank estimations for 2017 are
no better, they go from 1 to 2%”.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:48.160
El Mercurio.)

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.740
And despite all the growth of the last decades,
3 out of 4 Chileans make less than $700 a

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:54.740
month.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.290
So now you can probably understand the anger
- these are salaries that don´t belong to

00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.290
a rich country.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.120
&nbsp;
In other words, the Chilean model seems to

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.330
be failing, and there is someone responsible
for this slowdown: Michelle Bachelet, the

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:09.440
current president of Chile.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.960
(THE REVOLUTION OF BACHELET)

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.170
Michelle Bachelet was the president of Chile
from March 2006 to March 2010.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:23.560
But here, in Chile, a president can’t be
elected to two consecutive terms.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.710
So Michelle had to wait for 4 years before
she could run again for “La Moneda” - the

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.880
name of the Presidential Palace in Chile.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:34.280
Well, after gaining the support of almost
all the left-wing parties, including the Socialist

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:39.900
Party and the Communist Party, Bachelet won
the elections in November 2013 and a few months

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:47.319
later, in March 2014 she became the President
of the Republic of Chile, again.

00:03:47.319 --> 00:03:51.090
In this second term, Bachelet decided to go
through with reforms which were much more

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.910
aggressive than the ones she came up with
her first time around.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.260
&nbsp;
Her first decision was to increase taxation

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.290
on companies by almost 40%.

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:00.380
Quite the dramatic move…

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:04.810
She did it by raising the rates on profits
and, also, eliminating a bonus which meant

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:09.470
companies could reduce taxation if the profits
were reinvested in Chile.

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:15.700
If you think about it, that rule about reinvestment
was actually a really reasonable incentive…

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:20.709
A country like Chile which is far from being
rich needs factories, machines, businesses,

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:22.190
technology, and investment.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:25.510
And this incentive encouraged the growth of
all of these things…

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:30.440
So, what’s the point of punishing companies
providing this much needed investment?

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:34.850
And in addition to all this, taxation on companies
wasn’t exactly low to begin with.

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:38.400
Take a look at how things are now, after Bachelet
punished these companies:

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.970
“Chile is the second country with the highest
taxation on companies, relatively speaking,

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:47.020
only surpassed by Japan, a much richer country
with more consolidated companies”.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:48.020
OECD)

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:49.900
Would you like to know the consequences of
this measure?

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:54.620
Well, investment has fallen by 30% since Bachelet
came back to La Moneda.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:59.690
And, of course, less investment means less
jobs, less growth and, therefore, less tax

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:00.690
revenue.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:05.470
In other words, they decided to eat the cake
before they could even bake it, and, well,

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:06.690
that rarely works.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:11.630
That is why there has been a clear international
tendency to reduce taxation on companies over

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:14.350
the last few years.

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:21.780
Canada, the UK, Ireland are some of the examples
where corporate taxation has been reduced.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:27.639
The new president of France, Emmanuel Macron
has promised to copy those policies.

00:05:27.639 --> 00:05:30.160
Well, Chile is headed in the opposite direction.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.720
But wait a second, because that isn’t all.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:37.890
The other two main reforms Bachelet has put
through have resulted a very similar outcome.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:40.320
&nbsp;
Labour reform has made recruitment more expensive

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.150
and has given more power to trade unions.

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:44.889
&nbsp;
Some might like the sound of that, but it

00:05:44.889 --> 00:05:49.270
means more costs for companies which when
added to the increase on taxation means there

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:52.830
is even less investment, less competition
and less exports.

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:54.259
A whole lot of problems.

00:05:54.259 --> 00:05:57.090
&nbsp;
On the other hand, Michelle Bachelet tried

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:01.790
to pass an ambitious reform to turn the private
college education system into a public, free

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:03.110
of charge, service.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:07.220
The problem is, dear VisualPolitik viewer,
that this model is very expensive and some

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:11.690
say the solution to the problems of education
is to improve the quality of the universities

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:15.280
themselves instead of making them into factories
printing diplomas.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.040
Anyhow, the thing is that Bachelet couldn't
pass this reform, but in her attempt to do

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.590
so, she turned the whole education system
upside down.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:25.920
For example, she has reduced the investment
they had planned before that was set to improve

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:27.410
the quality of the private universities.

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:30.120
&nbsp;
But, ok, now you might be wondering: Well,

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.490
what do the citizens of Chile think about
all of this?

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:36.930
Well, look closely:
“62% of Chileans consider the government

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:43.870
of Michelle Bachelet must put off or at least
“moderate” the reforms which have been

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:48.460
forced in the country, especially the educational
reform” TeleSur.)

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:53.030
It feels as if Bachelet was working towards
an ideological change rather than a formula

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:58.970
which could actually solve national problems.

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:02.710
In fact, all these reforms and her hostility
towards private corporations have led Chile

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:05.780
to suffer the worst economic period for decades.

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:09.320
And, do you know who is most negatively affected
by this situation?

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:11.830
Well, that’s the poor and the working class...

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:18.540
After all, the wealthy already have a good
standard of living.

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:22.250
The thing is Bachelet leaves the country with
a crisis, with a growing public deficit, with

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:26.700
the highest public spending of the last 26
years, and with a damaged economy.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:30.870
But now that you know all of this, you might
be wondering: besides Bachelet, what are the

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:33.360
biggest weaknesses in Chile?

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.470
Well, let’s see:

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:40.199
(CHILE´S WEAKNESSES)

00:07:40.199 --> 00:07:46.620
It is a fact that Chile doesn't like foreign
professionals.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:50.600
It is a bit strange for a country with growth
like Chile, that they seem to have been incapable

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:52.860
of attracting foreign professionals.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:55.850
&nbsp;
Immigration is only 2%, and that is a problem;

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:58.949
it affects entrepreneurship, innovation and
trade.

00:07:58.949 --> 00:08:01.200
“Why won´t Chile grow?

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.690
Because it is full of Chileans!"

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:08.470
Ricardo Hausmann, Director of the Center for
International Development of Harvard University.

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:10.130
&nbsp;
Generally speaking, foreign professionals

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:14.220
tend be more entrepreneurial, which helps
the economy to diversify.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:20.330
“In Chile, the rate of entrepreneurship
of immigrants multiplies by four the local´s

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:21.330
rate”.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:26.060
A second problem is that the so-called “social
ladder” is not working.

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:31.270
That is there are no changes in social classes:
born poor, die poor, born rich, die rich.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:33.690
&nbsp;
In fact, the country is controlled by 7 families

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:37.550
whose wealth is equivalent to the 17% of the
national GDP.

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:42.119
This might seem unimportant, but when the
social ladder and competition don’t work,

00:08:42.119 --> 00:08:46.449
it means the family name is more important
than talent and effort.

00:08:46.449 --> 00:08:48.699
The opportunity cost for the country is huge.

00:08:48.699 --> 00:08:50.950
&nbsp;
And these families usually have quite special

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:52.880
relationships with the power.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:09:01.089
Consider this:
“Bachelet´s son brought to justice for

00:09:01.089 --> 00:09:02.089
a case of corruption.

00:09:02.089 --> 00:09:04.480
Andronico Luksic, one of the most powerful
men in Chile and the owner of the Bank of

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:06.930
Chile is also involved in the plot.”

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:09.699
Yep, the Bachelet has even been spoiled with
corruption...

00:09:09.699 --> 00:09:10.760
And then, there is the bureaucracy.

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:13.100
&nbsp;
I am sure you have heard that Chile is a very

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:14.100
free economy…

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:18.579
well, when you take a closer look you can
see it is also very bureaucratic and has all

00:09:18.579 --> 00:09:19.860
kinds of regulations.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:22.080
&nbsp;
For instance, Chile can´t import second hand

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:24.980
cars, and foreign workers don’t have access
to a bank account…

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:25.980
so…

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:28.759
well, it certainly could be more free!

00:09:28.759 --> 00:09:34.010
Mark Twain said there are three kinds of lies:
lies, damn lies, and statistics.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:37.350
The figures in Chile probably have some of
that…

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:43.759
The next elections in the country will take
place on the 19th of November 2017, and one

00:09:43.759 --> 00:09:50.499
thing is sure: if Chile wants to go back to
the good times, they must change direction.

00:09:50.499 --> 00:09:53.369
So, what do you think?

00:09:53.369 --> 00:09:54.990
What is your opinion of Bachelet?

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:56.750
What do you think of the Chilean model?

00:09:56.750 --> 00:10:02.199
Well, please leave your answers in the comments
below and click like if you enjoyed this video!

